---
import SplitText from './SplitText.astro';
import { interests } from '../data/site';
import ScopeFigure from './ScopeFigure.astro';
---

<section id="research" class="section" aria-labelledby="research-title">
  <div class="container-x">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-line" data-scramble>01 · Research</p>
      <SplitText as="h2" id="research-title" text="When the cause is inside the machine" />
    </div>

    <div class="reveal grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,40rem)_1fr] lg:gap-16">
      <div class="space-y-5 text-[1.0625rem]">
        <p>
          The first time both drive motors of a research robot I was building started together, its LiDAR went dark for about three
          seconds. Motor inrush had pulled the supply rail down by about 1.5&nbsp;V. The software had no way to know why it
          had stopped seeing, so it concluded that the room had emptied.
        </p>
        <ScopeFigure />
        <SplitText
          as="blockquote"
          effect="scroll"
          text="How should a robot reason about perception it can no longer trust, when the cause is inside the machine rather than in the room?"
          class="border-l-2 border-accent py-1 pl-5 font-serif text-xl leading-snug text-ink italic sm:text-2xl"
        />
        <p>
          Perception-aware planners predict how well a robot will sense from the scene around it. I want to add evidence
          from the robot’s own body, such as supply voltage, temperature and computing load, so the planner can avoid paths
          that depend on sensing it is about to lose.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="mb-4 font-sans text-sm font-semibold tracking-wide text-muted uppercase">Research interests</h3>
        <ul class="stagger flex flex-col gap-3">
          {
            interests.map((i, n) => (
              <li style={`--i:${n}`} class="rounded-md border border-line bg-surface px-4 py-3 text-[0.97rem] leading-snug">{i}</li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
